A||N||1||Dave85||Inner tie rod ends||||1||
Z||000000||Dave85||12-06-1999||09:50 PM||||<BR>I did a search and it didn't tell me much.  Batboy and Tom, I know you've done this.  Is this a major job or something that I can do?  It doesn't look too hard but you know how that goes.  How can you tell if the rack needs work or replaced.<BR>Thanks, Dave ||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||12-06-1999||10:34 PM||||First jack up the front end and put jack stands under it. Then loosen up the large jam nut a little bit that is between the outer and inner tie rod. Pry and pull the inner tie rod accordian boot clamp off. To reinstall the boot, I used large worm gear hose clamp, but some people use large nylon wire ties. Slip the boot towards the outer side. Loosen the inner tie rod using a large open wrench (don't remember the size, 1-1/4" maybe?). It'll took a little leverage to break it loose. Now take off the cotter pin and loosen the castellated nut until the top of the nut is even with the top of the tie rod stud. A couple good solid whacks with a heavy hammer ought to pop the outer tie rod loose. If not, you may have to use a pickle fork, but that usually damages the little rubber boot if you were planning on reusing the outer tie rod. Unscrew the outer tie rod off the inner tie rod while carefully counting the number of turns. Take the jam nut off and pull the accordian boot off, then unscrew the inner tie rod. Oops, forgot to say that on the passenger side, you need to take one side of the steering stablizer off. When you get to the point of installing the outer tie rod, screw it on the same number of turns that it took to remove. This will put your alignment back close enough to immediately drive to the shop for an alignment. Well, Tom or Ogre, did I forget anything?||||reg||1
